# Four notes, scaled to a letter: two for the default reader (linear viscoelastic laws, and elasticity with respect
# to a reference metric, the paper's key mechanism), two for students only. None in the introduction, which states
# the idea itself; each follows the first paragraph whose argument uses it. Listed in reading order.
BACKGROUND = [
    {"id": "bg-linear-ve", "after": "root-p4", "title": "Linear viscoelasticity and spring-dashpot models", "audience": ["student", "physicist"],
     "body": r"""<p>In linear viscoelasticity a material responds to a strain history by adding up the responses to each past increment of strain (Boltzmann superposition). The stress is then a convolution, $\sigma(t)=\int G(t-s)\dot\varepsilon(s)\,ds$, where the relaxation function $G(t)$ is the stress that follows a unit step of strain. Spring-dashpot models build such laws from ideal parts: a spring gives stress proportional to strain, a dashpot stress proportional to strain rate. A spring and dashpot in series (a Maxwell element) relax all stress, like a fluid. Adding a second spring in parallel gives the standard linear solid, whose stress relaxes from an instantaneous modulus to a smaller long-time one with a single time $\tau$. Every linear viscoelastic solid has such an instantaneous and a long-time stiffness. In the paper's form, $G(t)=C(1-\beta+\beta\phi(t))$: $\beta$ is the fraction of stiffness lost, and the kernel $\phi$ says how fast.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-metric-strain", "after": "root-p4", "title": "Strain from metrics", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>Label each material point by coordinates $x^i$ that move with the body. The metric $g_{ij}$ gives the length of a small material segment in the deformed body, $dl^2=g_{ij}dx^idx^j$; in terms of the deformation gradient $F$ it is $F^TF$. A rest reference metric $\bar g^0_{ij}$ gives the same segment's length in the stress-free state. Half their difference, $\varepsilon_{ij}=\tfrac12(g_{ij}-\bar g^0_{ij})$, is the Green–Lagrange strain: it vanishes under rigid rotations and measures stretching exactly even when rotations are large, as in a shell turned inside out. The stress that pairs with this strain in the elastic energy is the second Piola–Kirchhoff stress $S^{ij}$, defined in the material coordinates rather than in the lab. For small strains both reduce to the familiar linear strain and Cauchy stress. Because a metric is defined pointwise, the reference metric need not come from any shape, which is what lets the paper make it evolve.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-reference-metric", "after": "root-p5", "title": "Elasticity with respect to a non-Euclidean reference metric", "audience": ["student", "physicist"],
     "body": r"""<p>In ordinary elasticity the stress-free state is a shape in space, and strain is measured from it. Incompatible, or non-Euclidean, elasticity generalises this: each small piece of material carries its own preferred lengths, a reference metric $\bar g_{ij}$, and nothing requires that a body realising all of them at once exists in flat space. When the reference metric is Euclidean, with vanishing curvature, the body can relax to zero stress. When it is not, as in swelling gels or growing leaves, no configuration is stress-free; the body settles into the shape whose metric, necessarily Euclidean, is closest to $\bar g_{ij}$ in the sense of the elastic energy, and keeps residual stress. The equilibrium shape thus solves a minimisation over Euclidean metrics with the reference metric as data. The paper treats a viscoelastic body at each instant as such an elastic body, whose reference metric drifts in time.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-snap", "after": "root-p8", "title": "Elastic stability and snap-through", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>An elastic structure at rest sits at a local minimum of its elastic energy among the shapes it can take. Equilibria are the extrema of that energy, and an equilibrium has stability when the Hessian, the matrix of second derivatives of the energy, is positive definite, so that every small change raises the energy. Shells and arches can have two stable shapes, like a popper's natural and inverted forms. If a slowly changing parameter flattens the energy around one minimum until it merges with a nearby saddle, the Hessian gains a zero eigenvalue and the minimum disappears. Nothing then holds the structure, and it jumps, in a time set by elasticity and inertia, to another minimum: a snap-through. Before that moment the motion is slow and simply follows the parameter. In a delayed instability the slowly changing parameter is the creeping reference metric.</p>"""},
]
